<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  proctor-agent's own Info.plist — PRO-0040.

  This is NOT a bundle's Info.plist. It is linked into the proctor-agent binary as
  a __TEXT,__info_plist section (see the ProctorAgent target in Package.swift), so
  the agent presents its own identity instead of inheriting the enclosing app's.

  Why it exists. The agent is a second Mach-O in Proctor.app/Contents/MacOS, started
  by launchd, and it touches NSApplication on its first line to claim the accessory
  activation policy. That registers it with LaunchServices, and without this section
  it registered under the *app's* bundle identifier. `open -a Proctor` then found a
  live instance, activated the agent, and exited 0 having shown nothing — so Proctor
  could not be opened by any normal means while its own agent was running, which is
  nearly always. Double-clicking it in /Applications did nothing, with no error.

  What must not change. The signing identifier is NOT this. build-app.sh signs every
  nested binary with `-i app.fledgeling.procter`, and the TCC grants for Accessibility
  and Screen Recording match on the designated requirement, which names that signing
  identifier and the team and contains no path. Keeping the signature identical is
  what carries the grants across an upgrade instead of asking a person for them
  again — and Screen Recording cannot be granted silently on any macOS version, so
  re-prompting is a real cost to a person. A codesign run that omitted `-i` would
  take the identifier below as the signing identifier and lose the grants a release
  later, silently, so build-app.sh fails the build if the agent's signing identifier
  is ever anything but the app's.

  LSUIElement, not LSBackgroundOnly. LSBackgroundOnly is the plist form of the
  prohibited activation policy, which is documented as not permitting windows, and
  the agent draws the run HUD and the cursor overlay. LSUIElement is the accessory
  policy the agent already claims in code, declared early enough that LaunchServices
  reads it before the process starts rather than one statement later.

  No version keys, deliberately. The agent reports its build through BuildInfo
  (PRO-0030); a second version string here would be a second thing that can disagree
  with the tag scripts/check-release-version.sh gates on.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>app.fledgeling.procter.agent</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>Proctor Agent</string>
	<key>LSUIElement</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
